        Never use a hex code directly in a component — always use the token name.

## G2. Typography — Fonts (confirm exact family/weights from live site CSS)

  Likely candidates based on theme source (verify before locking in):
  --f-body:   Lato or similar sans-serif   → body text, nav, UI labels
  --f-head:   Millar or Enfold default     → headings (h1-h4)

  LOADING: use next/font (next/font/google or next/font/local for any non-Google
  font like Millar) — NOT a <link> tag or @import.

  src/app/layout.tsx — load fonts via next/font, assign as CSS variables.

## G3. Type Scale — measure exact px/line-height/letter-spacing per heading level
  from the live rendered pages before writing CSS. Do not approximate.

## G4. Border Radius, G5. Buttons, G6. Spacing, G7. Header/Nav, G8. Product Card,
  G9. Section backgrounds — same rule as G1/G3: measure from the live site,
  record exact values here as each section is built, never approximate.

## G10. Responsive Breakpoints (Tailwind — standard, no need to re-measure)

  sm:   640px   → small phones
  md:   768px   → tablets
  lg:   1024px  → desktop
  xl:   1280px  → wide desktop
  2xl:  1536px  → ultra wide (rarely needed)

  Mobile-first: write base styles for mobile, override at md: and lg:

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION H: ENVIRONMENTS & API SETUP
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## H1. Environment URLs

  BACKEND (single source for all environments — dev, preview, prod all point here):
    WordPress backend:   https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com
    WP REST base:        https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com/wp-json/wp/v2
    WC REST base:        https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com/wp-json/wc/v3
    WC Store API base:   https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com/wp-json/wc/store/v1

  FRONTEND:
    Next.js dev:         http://localhost:3000
    Next.js live:        Vercel (project URL set up in the Vercel steps — see DEPLOY.md)

  NOTE: WPGraphQL is NOT currently installed on this backend (confirmed by plugin
  audit). Default to the WordPress REST API + WooCommerce REST API / Store API.
  If GraphQL is wanted later, it requires installing WPGraphQL + WPGraphQL for
  WooCommerce first — treat that as a separate decision, not assumed.

## H2. Environment Variables — .env.local (never commit this file)

  # ── Backend URLs ──────────────────────────────────
  NEXT_PUBLIC_WP_URL=https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com

  # ── WooCommerce REST API keys (read-only) ─────────
  # Generate in: WP Admin → WooCommerce → Settings → Advanced → REST API
  # Permission: Read only (shop pages don't need write)
  WC_CONSUMER_KEY=ck_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
  WC_CONSUMER_SECRET=cs_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

  # ── Site config ───────────────────────────────────
  NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

  For production: duplicate above with live values in hosting env.
  For each env: LOCAL / PREVIEW / PRODUCTION — set separately.

## H3. API Strategy — WooCommerce REST API + WP REST API

  THIS PROJECT USES: WordPress REST API (wp/v2) + WooCommerce REST API (wc/v3)
  and/or WooCommerce Store API (wc/store/v1) for cart.

  WHY REST over GraphQL here: WPGraphQL is not installed on this backend. Adding
  it is a backend change that needs a decision first — don't assume it exists.

  - wc/v3   → server-side only (needs Consumer Key/Secret) — products, categories,
              full product data for listing/detail pages
  - wc/store/v1 → public, cookie-based cart — use for cart add/update/remove from
              client-side without exposing REST API keys in the browser
  - wp/v2   → pages, posts, media, menus (nav), global site content

## H4. REST client setup in Next.js

  PATTERN for server-side product/category calls (src/lib/woocommerce.ts):

    const WC_AUTH = Buffer.from(
      `${process.env.WC_CONSUMER_KEY}:${process.env.WC_CONSUMER_SECRET}`
    ).toString("base64");

    export async function getProducts(params = "") {
      const res = await fetch(
        `${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_WP_URL}/wp-json/wc/v3/products${params}`,
        {
          headers: { Authorization: `Basic ${WC_AUTH}` },
          next: { revalidate: 3600 },
        }
      );
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`WC products fetch failed: ${res.status}`);
      return res.json();
    }

  This code and ALL wc/v3 calls go in src/lib/woocommerce.ts — server-only file.
  NEVER call wc/v3 with the Consumer Key/Secret from a client component.

  PATTERN for cart (client-safe, Store API, src/lib/storeApi.ts):

    export async function addToCart(id: number, quantity: number) {
      const res = await fetch(
        `${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_WP_URL}/wp-json/wc/store/v1/cart/add-item`,
        {
          method: "POST",
          headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
          credentials: "include", // Store API uses a cart cookie/nonce
          body: JSON.stringify({ id, quantity }),
        }
      );
      return res.json();
    }

  Store API handles its own cart session via cookie + nonce — read the nonce
  from the first GET /wc/store/v1/cart response and send it back as
  `X-WC-Store-API-Nonce` on mutating requests.

## H5. Call rules — who fetches what

  GOLDEN RULE: 1 page = 1-2 fetch calls max. Components never fetch — they only
  receive props.

  ── Call pattern per Phase 1 page ───────────────────────────────────
  Homepage        → 1-2 calls (featured/bestseller products + nav/global data)
  Shop/Category   → 1 call (products list + categories, via query params)
  Product page    → 1 call (single product by slug/id, incl. variations)
  Cart page       → Store API, client-side, no server fetch needed on load if
                     cart state is read from the Store API cart cookie on mount
  Header + Footer → fetched once in layout.tsx (nav menu, footer content),
                     shared across all pages via layout, not re-fetched per page

  ── Who is allowed to fetch ─────────────────────────────────────────
  ✅ src/app/*/page.tsx          → server component, calls a lib/ function ONCE
  ✅ src/app/layout.tsx          → for nav/footer global data only
  ✅ src/hooks/use*.ts           → client-side mutations (cart)
  ❌ src/components/**/*         → NEVER — receives props only
  ❌ Inline in JSX               → NEVER — no fetch() inside return()

  ── Folder structure for API calls ──────────────────────────────────
  src/lib/
  ├── woocommerce.ts   ← server-only, wc/v3 calls (products, categories)
  ├── storeApi.ts      ← client-safe, wc/store/v1 calls (cart)
  ├── wordpress.ts      ← wp/v2 calls (pages, menus, posts if needed)
  └── formatPrice.ts    ← currency display logic

  Rule: every exported function in lib/ wraps fetch() with a clear name.
  No raw fetch() calls anywhere except inside lib/ files.

  ── Cache values by data type ────────────────────────────────────────
  { revalidate: 86400 } → 24 hours  — header/footer nav, global site content
  { revalidate: 3600  } → 1 hour    — shop listing, product detail, categories
  { cache: "no-store" } → always fresh — cart reads/mutations

  ── Parallel calls: only when truly independent ──────────────────────
  const [product, nav] = await Promise.all([
    getProductBySlug(slug),
    getGlobalNav(),
  ]);
  Never await one then the other if they don't depend on each other.

  ── Page file pattern (always thin) ──────────────────────────────────
  // src/app/shop/page.tsx
  import { getProducts } from "@/lib/woocommerce"
  import ProductGrid from "@/components/shop/ProductGrid"

  export default async function ShopPage() {
    const products = await getProducts()     // ← 1 call, all data
    return <ProductGrid products={products.data} />
  }
  // No logic here. No second fetch. Components get props, nothing else.

## H6. Dynamic Content Rule — nothing hardcoded that lives in WordPress

  RULE: Every piece of content that an admin should be able to change later
  (menu links, footer text, homepage hero/featured products, product data) must
  come from WordPress, not be hardcoded in a Next.js file.

  WHAT THIS MEANS for Phase 1 pages:
  - Header nav links          → WP Menu (wp/v2/menus or menu-items), not hardcoded array
  - Footer links/text         → WP Menu + a footer content source (page/ACF if needed)
  - Homepage featured/bestseller products → WooCommerce REST (category or featured flag)
  - Shop/category products    → WooCommerce REST, live data
  - Product page content      → WooCommerce REST (name, price, description, images,
                                 variations, stock) — never hardcode a product's copy
  - Cart                      → Store API live cart, never a hardcoded/sample cart

  NEVER DO THIS in any Next.js file:
    <h1>Welcome to ARK Equine</h1>              ← hardcoded = wrong if it should be editable
    const navLinks = ["Shop", "About", "Blog"]   ← hardcoded = wrong
    src="/images/hero-banner.jpg"                 ← hardcoded = wrong if admin uploads it

  ALWAYS DO THIS:
    <h1>{data.heroHeadline}</h1>                 ← from WP/WC data
    {menuItems.map(item => <NavLink ... />)}      ← from WP Menu
    <Image src={product.images[0].src} />         ← from WC product data

  EXCEPTION: pure UI chrome with no admin-editable meaning (e.g. "Add to Cart"
  button label, "Qty" label) can stay as plain text — it's UI copy, not content.

## H7. Confirming the setup works (first thing to do before any component work)

  STEP 1: Visit https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com/wp-json/wp/v2/types in browser
    → Should return JSON listing post, page, product, etc. = WP REST API is live ✓

  STEP 2: Visit https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com/wp-json/wc/v3/products with
    Basic Auth (Consumer Key/Secret) → should return product list = WC REST is live ✓

  STEP 3: Visit https://arkequine.getkgkrunch.com/wp-json/wc/store/v1/cart (no
    auth needed) → should return an empty cart object = Store API is live ✓

  STEP 4: If any fails → fix the backend (install/activate the right plugin or
    generate REST keys) before writing any Next.js code.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION A: PROJECT ARCHITECTURE RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## A1. Backend stays WordPress — never move logic to Next.js
- WordPress + WooCommerce = data, pricing, stock, order creation, plugin logic
- Next.js = display only (catalog, PDP, cart UI)
- Never replicate server-side plugin logic in Next.js:
  - Wholesale role pricing (WooCommerce Wholesale Prices/Premium)
  - Subscription pricing/billing (WooCommerce Subscriptions)
  - Multi-currency conversion (CURCY)
  - Discount/coupon rules (Discount Rules for WooCommerce, Advanced Coupons)
  - Min/max quantity validation (WooCommerce Min and Max Quantities)
  All of the above: read the already-calculated value from the WC REST/Store API
  response. Never compute a price or a discount in Next.js.

## A2. Checkout strategy — decide before Phase 2, not assumed in Phase 1
- Phase 1 scope stops at the Cart page. Checkout/payment flow (Stripe via
  Payment Plugins for Stripe WooCommerce) is a Phase 2 decision: either redirect
  to WordPress's own checkout page, or build a custom Next.js checkout against
  the Store API. Do not build checkout until this is explicitly decided.

## A3. Cart — Store API only, no custom cart logic duplicated server-side
- Cart state is whatever the WooCommerce Store API session says. Zustand (or
  equivalent) only mirrors it client-side for instant UI feedback — it is not
  the source of truth.
- Always carry the correct `id` (variation id for variable/subscription products,
  not the parent product id) when adding to cart.

## A4. Auth is out of scope for Phase 1
- Wholesale pricing requires a logged-in customer with the wholesale role. Phase
  1 shows guest/retail pricing only. Don't build login/JWT auth until it's
  scoped — that's Phase 2+ work.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION B: CODE QUALITY RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## B1. File size limit — 200 lines max
- Hard limit: 200 lines per file. Over 200 = split the file.
- Split by extracting: sub-components into their own files, helpers into lib/, hooks into hooks/
- Exception: auto-generated files (next.config.ts, etc.)

## B2. Component-first — no copy-paste markup
- Every repeating UI piece = its own component file
- One component per file. File name = component name (PascalCase)
- Even a small reusable button with special logic = its own file in src/components/ui/

## B3. Path comment — first line of every file
- Line 1 of every file must be a comment showing its own path (relative to src/)

  EXAMPLES:
    // src/components/shop/ProductCard.tsx
    // src/lib/woocommerce.ts
    // src/store/cart.ts
    // src/hooks/useCart.ts

## B4. Comments — simple English, one line, WHY not WHAT
- Write a comment only when the WHY is not obvious from the code
- Use plain simple English — any developer must understand it
- Maximum one short line per comment — no paragraph blocks
- Never describe what the code does. Describe why it does it.

  GOOD: // Store API needs the variation id, not the parent product id, for variable products
  GOOD: // WC returns a flat list; grouped here so the category filter UI works
  BAD:  // This function maps over the products array and returns JSX for each product
  BAD:  // set loading to true  (self-explanatory — no comment needed)

## B5. Folder structure — never mix concerns

  src/
  ├── app/          → routes only (page.tsx, layout.tsx, loading.tsx, error.tsx)
  ├── components/   → UI components, grouped by feature
  │   ├── layout/   → Header, Footer, Nav
  │   ├── shop/     → ProductCard, ProductGrid, CategoryFilter
  │   ├── cart/     → CartItem, CartSummary, CartDrawer
  │   ├── product/  → ProductImages, ProductInfo
  │   └── ui/       → Button, Badge, Spinner (generic reusables)
  ├── lib/          → API clients and pure utility functions
  ├── hooks/        → custom React hooks (useCart, useProduct, etc.)
  ├── store/        → Zustand stores only
  └── types/        → TypeScript interfaces only

  RULES:
  - app/        → calls components. NO inline JSX logic.
  - lib/        → pure functions. NO React, NO hooks, NO JSX.
  - components/ → receives props. NO direct fetch() calls.
  - hooks/      → state and side effects. Calls lib/ for data.

## B6. Naming conventions

  Component file   → PascalCase          ProductCard.tsx
  Hook file        → use + camelCase     useCart.ts
  Lib/util file    → camelCase           woocommerce.ts, formatPrice.ts
  Store file       → camelCase           cart.ts
  Type/Interface   → PascalCase          Product, CartItem, WCVariation
  Constant         → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE    WC_API_URL, REVALIDATE_SHOP
  CSS              → Tailwind utilities only, no custom class names

## B7. Page files must be thin — no logic inside
- page.tsx = fetch data (if needed) + pass to component. Nothing else.
- No business logic, no long JSX trees, no inline styles in page files.

## B8. TypeScript — no `any`, ever
- All API response types in src/types/
- Write the interface first, then write the function
- If type is unknown: use `unknown` and narrow it — never `any`

## B9. Server Components by default — `"use client"` only when required
- Default: Server Component (no directive needed)
- Add "use client" ONLY when the component uses: useState, useEffect, event handlers, Zustand
- Cart, interactive buttons, drawers = client components
- Product pages, shop listing, homepage sections = server components (faster load)

## B10. Environment variables — two strict categories

  NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix  → visible in browser + server  → safe to expose (site URL, WP URL)
  no prefix            → server only                   → secrets (WC keys)

  NEVER use a secret key inside a Client Component ("use client" file)
  API calls with secret keys → lib/ functions → called from Server Components or Route Handlers only

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION C: ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH (mandatory)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## C1. Each concern has exactly one home

  WooCommerce REST calls (server) → src/lib/woocommerce.ts   (only file using WC keys)
  WooCommerce Store API (cart)    → src/lib/storeApi.ts
  WordPress REST calls            → src/lib/wordpress.ts     (menus, pages)
  Cart state                      → src/store/cart.ts        (Zustand, mirrors Store API)
  Price formatting                → src/lib/formatPrice.ts

## C2. No scattered fetch() calls
- If two places need the same data → extract to lib/ and import
- Never duplicate an API call across components or pages

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION D: PERFORMANCE RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## D1. ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration) for catalog pages
- Shop/category listing  → revalidate: 3600   (rebuild every 1 hour)
- Product detail page    → revalidate: 3600
- Homepage sections      → revalidate: 86400  (rebuild every 24 hours)
- Header/Footer nav      → revalidate: 86400
- Cart                   → no cache, "use client", real-time only

## D2. Images — next/image only, never <img>
- Every image = <Image> from "next/image"
- Add the WordPress domain to next.config.ts images.remotePatterns
  (arkequine.getkgkrunch.com)
- Always set width + height (or fill) to avoid layout shift

## D3. Fonts — next/font only, never a <link> tag
- Load all fonts via next/font/google or next/font/local
- No Google Fonts <link> in layout.tsx
- This eliminates font-related layout shift and speeds up LCP

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION I: LOADING STATES & SKELETONS (MANDATORY — never ship a page without one)
# Rule: a click must NEVER look ignored. The page changes at once, and grey
#       skeleton holds the layout until the real data arrives.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## I1. The contract — this is not optional

  User clicks ANY link
      → the new page opens IMMEDIATELY (never wait on the old page)
      → its skeleton shows while the data loads
      → the real content replaces the skeleton in the SAME layout

  Never allowed:
  - A blank white screen while data loads
  - The old page frozen on screen after a click
  - A bare spinner in the middle of an empty page
  - An empty div used as a placeholder (e.g. <div className="min-h-[380px]" />)
  - "No products found" / "Your cart is empty" shown BEFORE the data has loaded

## I2. Every route needs a loading.tsx — no exceptions

  src/app/<route>/loading.tsx  → renders that route's skeleton component

  A route is NOT done until its loading.tsx exists. This is part of the task,
  not a polish step for later. Add it in the SAME step as the page.

  Phase 1 routes that need one:
    src/app/loading.tsx            (homepage)
    src/app/shop/loading.tsx
    src/app/shop/[category]/loading.tsx
    src/app/product/[slug]/loading.tsx
    src/app/cart/loading.tsx

## I3. Client-side waits need a skeleton too

  loading.tsx only covers server navigation. ALSO show a skeleton when:
  - Reading the cart from the Store API / localStorage (first client render)
  - A fetch inside a hook is still running
  - A "use client" component has data that is not ready yet

  Pattern:
    const hasMounted = useHasMounted();
    if (!hasMounted) return <XyzSkeleton />;   // never an empty div

## I4. Skeleton must MATCH the real layout, pixel for pixel

- Copy the real component's wrapper classes: grid columns, gaps, padding, min-height
- Same number of placeholder rows/cards as the page normally shows
- Nothing may jump or resize when real data lands (zero layout shift)
- Vary the bar widths (w-20 / w-24 / w-16) so it reads like text, not a barcode

## I5. Build skeletons from shared primitives — never hand-roll

  src/components/ui/Skeleton.tsx   → one grey shimmering block (size via className)
  src/components/ui/Spinner.tsx    → small ring, ONLY inside buttons

  Rules:
  - Never write a raw animated grey <div>; always use <Skeleton />
  - One skeleton file per component, named <Component>Skeleton.tsx,
    living next to the component it mirrors
  - Skeleton files are Server Components — no "use client", no props, no logic

## I6. Buttons that start work must say so

- A button that triggers a request switches to a pending label + <Spinner />
  (e.g. "Add to Cart" → "Adding…", "Update Cart" → "Updating…")
- Disable it while pending so it cannot be double-clicked
- NEVER let a pending state change an element's width in the header or nav —
  that makes the menu twitch. Colour/label only, or position the spinner absolutely.

## I7. Accessibility — a skeleton is not just a picture

- Wrap each skeleton block in role="status" + aria-label="Loading <what>"
- Put aria-busy={isLoading} on a box whose numbers are being re-checked
- Every <Skeleton /> is aria-hidden already — do not announce each bar
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion

## I8. Never fake slowness to "show" the skeleton

- No artificial setTimeout, no disabling prefetch, no removing ISR caching
- A fast page that skips the skeleton is CORRECT behaviour, not a bug
- To test one: DevTools → Network → Slow 4G, or clear .next/cache and cold-load

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION E: WHAT NOT TO BUILD IN NEXT.JS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## E1. Do NOT rebuild these — they run server-side and stay in WordPress
- Wholesale role price calculation (WooCommerce Wholesale Prices)
- Subscription pricing/billing (WooCommerce Subscriptions)
- Multi-currency conversion (CURCY)
- Discount/coupon rule calculation (Discount Rules for WooCommerce, Advanced Coupons)
- Min/max quantity validation (WooCommerce Min and Max Quantities)
- Stock/back-in-stock logic (Back In Stock Notifier)
- SEO meta calculation (Rank Math) — Next.js only reads and renders it
- Order creation / payment processing (stays on WordPress checkout for now — A2)

## E2. Deferred to a later phase (do not build in Phase 1)
- Checkout page / payment flow
- Account / login / wholesale registration
- Customer reviews widget (Customer Reviews Collector / Trustindex)
- Popups (Popup Maker, Boxzilla, Adoric)
- Live chat widget (Tidio)
- Contact form
